                <span class="badge-progress">in progress</span>
                <p class="hero-label">Aerospace · Propulsion &amp; Controls</p>
                <h1>Thrust Vector<br />Control Rocket</h1>
                <p class="hero-desc">
                    Building a 4"-diameter model rocket with an active
                    thrust-vector-control system, aiming toward a fully
                    propulsive vertical landing — the Falcon 9 booster
                    concept, built at hobbyist scale from COTS and
                    3D-printed parts. A Teensy 4.1 flight computer drives a
                    two-axis gimbaled motor mount through a PID loop reading
                    attitude from an onboard IMU.
                </p>
                <div class="skill-tags">
                    <span class="skill-tag">Python</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">PID Control</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">Monte Carlo Simulation</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">Siemens NX</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">NXOpen / Python</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">Embedded (Teensy 4.1)</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">OpenRocket</span>
                </div>
            </section>

            <p class="section-label">Problem / Goal</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    The goal is a fully propulsive vertical landing at
                    hobbyist scale — the same concept as a Falcon 9 booster
                    return, built from commercial-off-the-shelf and
                    3D-printed parts rather than an orbital-class vehicle.
                    That means a flight computer that can read the rocket's
                    attitude in real time and actively correct it through a
                    gimbaled motor mount, rather than relying on fins for
                    passive stability.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The long-term architecture uses two solid motors stacked
                    in series rather than a throttleable engine: an ascent
                    motor for launch and a separate landing motor for the
                    descent burn. Because the motors are fixed-thrust,
                    control comes from <em>timing</em> — the flight computer
                    has to calculate the ignition altitude for the landing
                    burn dynamically at apogee — rather than from
                    throttling. The project is informed by an existence
                    proof: a hobbyist ("Project Horizon") flew a rocket
                    called Eagle using the same motor class and the same
                    flight-computer architecture (Teensy 4.1) and achieved a
                    successful propulsive landing after 30 iterative
                    attempts.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Simulation</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    Before committing to hardware, I ran a simulation to
                    establish that the core concept was physically achievable —
                    that a flight computer could detect apogee, compute a
                    landing-burn ignition point, and fire in time. I built the
                    simulation with Claude's help, supplying the digitized F15
                    motor thrust curve, rough CP/CG estimates, and notes on how
                    mass and inertia change as propellant burns.
                </p>
                <p>
                    From that, a control law: sweeping possible apogee
                    altitudes and solving for the minimum safe ignition altitude
                    at each one produces a linear fit (h<sub>b</sub> = m·Ap + c)
                    that runs as firmware — every flight measures its own apogee
                    and computes its own ignition altitude rather than flying to
                    a fixed number.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A stochastic layer then ran the simulation hundreds of times
                    with randomized variation added each run — IMU sensor noise,
                    motor burn scatter, crosswind gusts — to see how sensitive
                    landing performance is to each individual variable. Those
                    parameter sweeps showed landing velocity is far more
                    sensitive to IMU accelerometer bias than to servo speed,
                    which shifted engineering focus toward sensor calibration
                    over faster actuators.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The simulation was enough proof of concept to start building.
                    Partway through hardware design I pivoted the recovery
                    approach — TVC-controlled ascent with a parachute landing
                    rather than a full propulsive landing — to keep the timeline
                    realistic. The gimbal, flight computer, and control loop
                    stayed the focus.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Stage 1 — Gimbal &amp; Body Tube</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    A gimbal is 2 rotational degrees of freedom. The first real
                    constraint was servo selection — MG90S servos had a track
                    record in similar projects but run large, which set a floor
                    on the rocket's diameter.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The CAD work here was almost entirely a dimensioning problem:
                    the inner and outer rings had to be structurally solid enough
                    to survive an Estes F15-0's thrust without snapping, while
                    never colliding with each other through their full range of
                    rotation, while also being thick enough to mount the servos
                    themselves. The motor casing was designed to friction-fit the
                    motor — a decision I'd revisit later, since it doesn't give a
                    consistent attachment position and the ejection charge risks
                    disrupting the gimbal's connection to the avionics bay.
                </p>
