            <p class="section-label">Stage 2 — Avionics Bay</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    The avionics bay covers the sensors, flight computer, buck
                    converters, and battery, plus the physical question of how
                    all of it mates together and secures to the airframe.
                    Perfboards made sense for cost and timeline at this stage.
                    Brass inserts secure the perfboards into the bay, and long
                    structural bars plus two threaded rods tie the bulkheads to
                    the bay for stiffness.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/03-avionics-bench-test.jpeg"
                            alt="Bench testing avionics bay control of the servo-driven nose cone"
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">Bench testing avionics bay control of the servo-driven nose cone before the SRM ever went near it.</p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    The original design used a bulkhead as the parachute's
                    attachment point; after a redesign, the nose cone became the
                    only attachment point instead. Barometer fusion for altitude
                    was planned but pushed to a later revision — pressurizing the
                    airframe correctly for a barometer to read cleanly turned out
                    to be its own problem.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Print settings got tuned specifically for structural stability
                    on this part: speed, nozzle temp, overhang-based speed,
                    support settings, seam positioning, wall loop count, sparse
                    infill pattern, and material choice between PETG and PLA
                    Basic.
                </p>
                <figure class="image-figure">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/tvc-rocket/08-avionics-accessibility.jpeg"
                            alt="Working the avionics bay wiring in the field"
                            loading="lazy"
                        />
                        <p class="caption">Working the avionics bay wiring in the field. This is where it became clear accessibility needs to be designed in from the start — everything here is reachable only by hand-threading wires through a tight opening.</p>
                    </div>
                </figure>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Stage 3 — Nose Cone</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    Started with a purchased nose cone to de-risk the timeline —
                    no aero CFD needed when you're copying a commercial body tube
                    and cone. Switched to a 3D-printed cone later to save mass
                    and cost, copying the commercial cone's dimensions in PLA.
                </p>
                <p>
                    That's when the CG problem showed up. The avionics bay is
                    the only component in the rocket with a CG you can actually
                    move, and once it became the parachute's attachment point,
                    its position stopped being a free variable — it had to sit
                    somewhere that made sense for both CG placement and chute
                    deployment, which meant either redoing the CG/CP
                    calculations or redesigning around a different attachment
                    scheme. This also exposed a broader issue: there's no
                    consistent method yet for inserting and securing components
                    inside the body tube, which raises open questions about
                    fastener choice and how repeatable the assembly is across
                    builds.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The nose cone ended up servo-powered, splitting into three
                    parts (base, left, right) that pop open when the
                    accelerometer detects a specific negative acceleration — the
                    vehicle is falling. The split is off-center on purpose, both
                    to preserve aerodynamic shape and so incoming airflow doesn't
                    just force the two halves apart. The parachute attaches
                    through two eyebolts running through the nose cone bulkhead.
                </p>
                <div class="gallery gallery-3">
